{"name":"Bone China","hex":"#f3edde","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bone china","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["an English form of porcelain made from clay mixed with bone ash"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bone_china"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bone china","blocks":[{"text":"Bone china is a type of vitreous, translucent pottery, the raw materials for which include bone ash, feldspathic material and kaolin. It has been defined as \"ware with a translucent body\" containing a minimum of 30% of phosphate derived from calcined animal bone or calcium phosphate. Bone china is amongst the strongest of whiteware ceramics, and is known for its high levels of whiteness and translucency. Its high strength allows it to be produced in thinner cross-sections than other types of whiteware. Like stoneware, it is vitrified, but is translucent due to differing mineral properties."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/BLW_Bone_China_Chocolate_Cup.jpg/330px-BLW_Bone_China_Chocolate_Cup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone_china","wikidata":"Q619865"},{"title":"Bone","blocks":[{"text":"A bone is a rigid organ that constitutes part of the skeleton in most vertebrate animals. Bones protect the organs of the body, produce red and white blood cells, store minerals, help regulate acid-base homeostasis, provide structure and support for the body, and enable mobility and hearing. Bones come in a variety of shapes and sizes and have complex internal and external structures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Left_femur_of_extinct_elephant%2C_Alaska%2C_Ice_Age_Wellcome_L0057714.jpg/330px-Left_femur_of_extinct_elephant%2C_Alaska%2C_Ice_Age_Wellcome_L0057714.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone","wikidata":"Q265868"},{"title":"China","blocks":[{"text":"China, officially the People's Republic of China (PRC), is a country in East Asia. It is divided into 33 province-level divisions, including two special administrative regions. Beijing is the capital, while Shanghai is the most populous city by urban area. Its geography features the Central Plain, major rivers such as the Yangtze and Yellow River, deserts, subtropical and temperate forests, plateaus, and mountain ranges such as the Himalayas. It is the world's second-most populous country after India, with a population exceeding 1.4 billion. Covering 9.6 million square kilometers (3,700,000 sq mi), it is the third largest country by area."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China","wikidata":"Q148"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Reagan-343.jpg/330px-Reagan-343.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Reagan-343.jpg/330px-Reagan-343.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plate from Ronald Reagan's state service for the White House, by Lenox","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReagan-343.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Bertazzo_S_-_SEM_deproteined_bone_-_wistar_rat_-_x10k.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Bertazzo_S_-_SEM_deproteined_bone_-_wistar_rat_-_x10k.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Bertazzo_S_-_SEM_deproteined_bone_-_wistar_rat_-_x10k.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Bertazzo_S_-_SEM_deproteined_bone_-_wistar_rat_-_x10k.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABertazzo_S_-_SEM_deproteined_bone_-_wistar_rat_-_x10k.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Blausen_0229_ClassificationofBones.png/500px-Blausen_0229_ClassificationofBones.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Blausen_0229_ClassificationofBones.png/1280px-Blausen_0229_ClassificationofBones.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Blausen_0229_ClassificationofBones.png/500px-Blausen_0229_ClassificationofBones.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One way to classify bones is by their shape or appearance.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlausen_0229_ClassificationofBones.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/603_Anatomy_of_Long_Bone.jpg/500px-603_Anatomy_of_Long_Bone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/603_Anat